A||||20||Formula88||MAF for a Fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||11-29-2001||10:45 PM||||Ok, so I'm reading stuff on the GM forum about the X-11 and early development of the V6HO that would become the loveable L44 V6 in our Fieros.  In 85, when the Citation V6 went from carb to MPFI, they used a Mass Air Flow setup instead of the Fiero's speed density (MAP and MAT sensors) setup.  Supposedly, the MAF setup is more tolerant to modifications, especially bigger cams.  Now, according to an X-11 website detailing the developement of the engine, the X-11 engine was the same as the Fiero engine.  Same block, heads, cam, and lower intake, but the upper plenum on the X-11 was different due to the MAF setup.  Has anyone ever thought about swapping a MAF setup onto a Fiero?  Don't know if it could be done on our plenum, which would be a pity, but it might make tuning and mods easier, especially for people with 3.4 pushrod engines running bigger cams.  I would think the ECM from the X-11 could be used as well, if you're running a stick shift.<P>Whaddya think?  Worth investigating?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Dave Gunsul||11-29-2001||11:33 PM||||I was exploring this option earlier this year and I even emailed a couple-a the X-11<BR>websites but I got no response from them.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>It would work better for our cars but I can't get a response form anyone that knows about the X-11's to compare notes with so I have given it up for now.<BR>If you find out anything please let me know<BR>because i'm very interested in pursuing this <BR>too! Feel free to contact me offlist if you want to.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 11-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Neal||11-29-2001||11:34 PM||||if i had the oppurtunity id try the MAF in a second.  I bet you could even use the fiero upper intake with a little modification.  A good MAF setup will be infinitly more accomidating to mods, big cams, exaust, port work... as wild as you want the computer can compensate.  some restrictions like fuel delivery, but much easier to realize benifits than with a MAP.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||11-29-2001||11:51 PM||||Sounds like it's time for some research.<P>Ok, I'll start here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Does anyone know offhand what other engines came with MAF that are "Fiero compatible"?<P>The 3.4 F-body is, right?  So in theory, we could use it's intake and DIS with it's ECM, correct?<P>How about the 3.1 MPFI?  There's a gazillion of them in junkyards.  Finding parts shouldn't be hard, and the upper intakes with MAF, etc. should bolt right up, I think.<P>If I find anything out from other sites, I'll update it here.  This definately sounds like the way to go on a 3.4 pushrod conversion.  If it can be done and keep the "Fiero" upper plenum, even better!  To me, that's the only reason to even consider the 3.4 pushrod.  If you're gonna loose the original looking engine, you might was well dump something else in it.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Formula88||11-29-2001||11:52 PM||||Oh, one interesting tidbit I found out was the reason the Chevy used MAF and the Fiero had Speed Density setup was as a backup.  If one failed Federal emissions certification, they could always use the other.  But since both passed, they just went with it.  ||||reg||7
Z||000005||DRH||11-30-2001||12:19 AM||||I believe the MAF is just a sensor that measures airflow upstream of the throttle body. It shouldn't be THAT hard to get one mounted between the air cleaner and throttle body. Wiring shouldn't be too bad. <P>You will also need a different ECM. Does the ECM have to be for a distributor or will one originally for DIS work??? (This could severely limit the options).<P>I believe the downside to MAF is that it restricts airflow. Probably not enough to worry about though.<P>Maybe terryk will jump in here soon.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||11-30-2001||08:49 AM||||Well, the MAF is probably no more restrictive than the Fiero's throttlebody, but even so, there are plenty of aftermarket sources for oversized MAF's that could flow enough for the Space Shuttle.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||11-30-2001||11:16 AM||||"No it's not a Citation!" website is mine. I used to own several X-11's and did all of the research on the car and the two ECM systems. <P>The MPFI mass (using a 6870) can handle 255 gm/s of air. I don't think too many Fiero's need to live in fear of reaching that number. That's 5.7L/3800SC territory.<P>Too bad Cliff doesn't have a file area. I have a wiring cross-reference between the 6870 and 6869/7170 ECM. Pretty close.<BR> <BR>URL deleted. I remembered that my ISP crashed their server and I never uploaded the webpage again. It's being used elsewhere.<P>TK<P><BR>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-30-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||thomas_l||11-30-2001||11:36 AM||||I had an 80 X-11 so it had the carb. It was a real fun, fast car and I only had to get the (4-speed) transaxle bearings done twice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>On the topic of mid/late 80's MAF retrofit: I seem to remember doing a LOT of recall updates on cars with the dual systems. The "kit" consisted of a MAP-only prom and directions to disable the MAF. I just don't remember what the official reason was for the change, other than the Motorola MAF sensors being junk.<P>Any comments?||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||11-30-2001||12:23 PM||||That recall was later on the MPFI engines. The masses were freaking out and causing drivability problems. You'll see lots of Beretta's with the Speed Density upgrade label in the engine compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Joe Torma||11-30-2001||01:13 PM||||You don't want a MAF.<BR>Its a $350 POS.<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||11-30-2001||02:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B>You don't want a MAF.<BR>Its a $350 POS.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What makes you say that? <P>Lots of cars use them without any problems, including the Z06 Corvette, LS1 F-bodies, and the 3400SFI in the new N-bodies.  It's design seems superior to the Speed Density setup, IF the parts are of good quality.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Formula88||11-30-2001||02:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>"No it's not a Citation!" website is mine. I used to own several X-11's and did all of the research on the car and the two ECM systems. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Very cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I remember when the X-11 first came out.  Motor Trend fell in love with the car.  I was all about the Trans Am at the time, so I didn't give it a second look, but in retrospect, it was way ahead of it's time.<P>I don't remember if it was on your site or not, but I did get a chuckle out of one X-11 owner's comment: "You may have more horsepower, but NOTHING can outhandle an X-11!"  I'd wager there are some Fieros on this board that would take that challenge.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||terryk||11-30-2001||03:08 PM||||There are so many of those 6870 masses in the junkyards, you could keep a gross in the trunk.||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||11-30-2001||03:13 PM||||"I don't remember if it was on your site or not, but I did get a chuckle out of one X-11 owner's comment: "You may have more horsepower, but NOTHING can outhandle an X-11!" I'd wager there are some Fieros on this board that would take that challenge. "<P>Actually, that was me. It was in joking reference to a friend with a Fiero at the time. We used to argue about which handled better. It came down to which car would leave the road faster and we agreed it would be a wash. X-11's stick like mad and then abruptly disappear into the bushes. Just stay in the throttle. Fiero's either plow or spin in a circle. It was all a friendly joke.<P>I had an Eibach'd/Koni'd 81 X-11 and I would take the challenge. It's just that when you reach the limit, there is no recovery.<P>Notice the word "transexual" for transaxle? I never did fix that. Great spelling correction. <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Joe Torma||11-30-2001||04:24 PM||||Because they are tempermental and cost $350 when they go bad.  New motors usually have BOTH a MAF and MAP sensor, which is decent.<BR>Only ok for limited upgrades to motor.<P>A map is a vacuum guage...a direct indicator of load.  Provides instant response when you hit the gas...every MAF only vehicle I've been in has rotten throttle response.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> What makes you say that? <P>Lots of cars use them without any problems, including the Z06 Corvette, LS1 F-bodies, and the 3400SFI in the new N-bodies.  It's design seems superior to the Speed Density setup, IF the parts are of good quality.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||MR2KLR||11-30-2001||06:02 PM||||I have driven both a 87 LT1 IROC camaro and a 2001 Ram Air Trans Am neither had poor anything! and both use MAS air flow sensors/Meters however my $70 MAP went bad 4 times in my last fiero I finaly just ran it in "Limp Home" mode the only bad part in a MAS air syste it that there is no "Limp Home" mode.  <P>PS my friend had a late 80's 2.8L Camaro that had a MAS air meter and no MAP. perhaps that ECM could run a Fiero with a MAS air meter||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||11-30-2001||06:36 PM||||Mass ECM's have limp home mode. It's just more like "horribly crippled and bruised" mode.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Dave Gunsul||12-01-2001||05:00 AM||||So Terry please tell us are you saying that <BR>the mass system is adaptable to the Fiero but we have to use the computer and meter from the X-11? Do we have to use the X-11's<BR>intake or can the Fiero intake be retained?<BR>Are there any other changes that need to be made?<BR>For Gods sake man spill your guts!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000019||Formula88||12-01-2001||01:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Actually, that was me. It was in joking reference to a friend with a Fiero at the time. We used to argue about which handled better. It came down to which car would leave the road faster and we agreed it would be a wash. X-11's stick like mad and then abruptly disappear into the bushes. Just stay in the throttle. Fiero's either plow or spin in a circle. It was all a friendly joke.<P>I had an Eibach'd/Koni'd 81 X-11 and I would take the challenge. It's just that when you reach the limit, there is no recovery.<P>Notice the word "transexual" for transaxle? I never did fix that. Great spelling correction. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Cool.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I've never driven an X-11, but had a Citation, Skylark, and Phoenix at one time or another.  It's hard to imagine being able to do enough mods to that suspension to make it handle as well as a Fiero. (base suspension).  Makes me wanna find an old X-11 now - just for being different.  And I'd want to find an old carbed version.  <P>So the X-11 sticks like crazy, then just leave the road, huh?  Not very user friendly.  At least the Fiero can be balanged on the ragged edge, but boy is it like balancing on a razor's edge!  The transition from understeer to oversteer sure is a short one!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Got KYB's on the Fiero now.  Can't wait for poly, lowering springs, and 17/18" rims.  FUN!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||terryk||12-01-2001||02:02 PM||||You get some warning, but not much. At the same time, you are well beyond what many FWD cars will do. When the front starts to push a little you are getting close to the limit. Of course you're smashed against one of the doors too. X-11's were a blast to drive. They aren't Vettes though (let's not go there).<P>Ok, the mass stuff. The mass MPFI engine was used in 85 X body and tons of 85-86 A body. The Celebrity MPFI cars with W engines is the best source for the parts if you want to try this. <P>Next, the only different in the engines between a Fiero 9 and an A/X W is the upper plenum and throttle body. The Holly TB will work with the mass ECM. No real difference anyway. Looks cool I guess.<P>Given that, the 6870 ECM will plug in, but a couple of wires need to be added or moved. I have a list if someone wants to see it.<P>You need to add the mass relay too. I don't see why you couldn't add the needed stuff and be able to swap between the two in an hour or so. You might want to consider making an adapter to switch between them while you play. ||||reg||1
